# Okta Kit for Gravwell — Overview

Detections, alerts, dashboards and investigation tools for the Okta System Log. The
[Integration Guide](https://docs.gravwell.io/integrations/application/okta.html) covers
getting Okta data into Gravwell.

***

## 1. Data and configuration

| Config macro | Default | Required | Contents |
|---|---|---|---|
| `$OKTA` | `okta` | yes | Okta System Log events, one JSON event per entry |
| `$OKTA_USERS` | `okta-users` | no | Okta user profile records (feed the user aggregates below and the user tables; each user counted once, from their newest record) |

Events per day in the System Log tag:

```query
tag=$OKTA json eventType | stats count over 24h | chart count
```

Event types in your data that the kit's `okta_eventTypes` lookup doesn't describe yet
(they still work in detections, they just have no category):

```query
tag=$OKTA json eventType | lookup -v -r okta_eventTypes eventType eventType category | stats count by eventType | sort by count desc | table eventType count
```

### How the dashboards get their data

Most dashboards search the System Log directly: General, Category, Event Type, Actor/Target,
Sign-In Locations, Threat Overview and Investigation. They show whatever history is in the tag
as soon as the kit is installed. So do all 49 detections.

The user-directory dashboards work differently. Searching every user record on each page load
is slow, and the user tag only holds what the ingester wrote recently.

- An hourly scheduled search, **Okta - User Aggregates** (Automation > Scheduled Searches),
  computes the directory figures from the newest record per user in the last 25 hours. It saves
  them to the resource `okta_user_aggregates` with `table -save -update rowkey`, as
  `ts,metric,key,value,rowkey` rows: the current count of every metric (`rowkey` = `metric|key`,
  overwritten each run) and one history point per run for last sign-in and password age
  (`rowkey` = `ts|metric|key`, never pruned). A key that no longer occurs keeps its last count.
- **Okta User Overview** (totals, status, user type, division, department, job title,
  manager), **Okta User Authentication Overview** (except its user table) and the timezone
  table on **Okta Sign-In Locations** read only that resource, with
  `dump -r okta_user_aggregates metric=="<metric>" key value`. They load instantly; only the two
  "over Time" charts depend on the time range.
- Detections never read the aggregates.

When each metric was last computed, how long ago, and how many rows the resource holds for it:

```query
dump -r okta_user_aggregates ts metric | stats max(ts) as newest count as rows by metric | eval age_minutes = (unix(NOW) - int(newest)) / 60; | table metric newest age_minutes rows
```

**What to expect right after installing:**

- **The scheduled search runs once at install, then at minute 17 of every hour.** The user
  dashboards are empty until the first run that finds user records. Run it by hand from
  Automation > Scheduled Searches to fill them sooner; an extra run only adds one more point to
  the "over Time" charts.
- **A full directory needs the hosted ingester's daily snapshot.** The ingester writes every
  user at startup and then once a day (versions after 1.0.0). Until it has done so, and always
  with version 1.0.0, the figures only cover users whose records changed in the last 25 hours.
  "Total Users" then reads low.
- **History starts at install.** The aggregates aren't backfilled, so the "over Time" charts
  start as a single point and gain one per hour; nothing removes old points. Upgrading the kit
  replaces the resource with the empty shipped copy, so history starts again after an upgrade.
- **Old figures mean missed runs.** If the search's owner can't write resources, or the user
  tag stops receiving data, the dashboards keep showing the last figures written. Check
  `age_minutes` in the query above (it should stay under about 60), and check the search's last
  run in Automation > Scheduled Searches.
- **Keys that disappear keep their last count.** A department, title, manager or status that no
  user has any more stays in the resource with the count it last had, and is included in the
  totals. Its `ts` stops advancing, which shows it is no longer current.

***

## 2. Alerting

All six alerts and their 49 scheduled searches are enabled at install. With no consumers, an
alert only writes its events to `_alerts`, the tag shared by every kit's alerts; nothing is sent
anywhere. The Okta kit's events have `Contents._vendor` set to `Okta`.

1. **Consumers**: to be notified, open an alert (Automation > Alerts) and add a consumer flow
   (email, Slack, Teams, webhook, ticket). The kit ships none because every team routes
   alerts differently.
2. **Tuning**: add your administrators and service accounts to the lookups in section 5.
3. **Turning detections off**: each scheduled search is one detection (labels `okta`,
   `detection`, and one group label: `authentication-sessions`, `admin-configuration-changes`,
   `account-mfa-changes`, `threats-platform`, `modification-feed`). Disable one to turn off that
   detection; disable an alert and its scheduled searches to turn off a group.

Every detection runs every 15 minutes over the previous 15 minutes. Start minutes are
spread across the quarter hour. Detections that compare with history look further back
on their own (up to 30 days).

| Alert | Detections |
|---|---|
| Alert - Okta - Authentication & Sessions | 24 |
| Alert - Okta - Admin & Configuration Changes | 13 |
| Alert - Okta - Account & MFA Changes | 5 |
| Alert - Okta - Threats & Platform | 5 |
| Alert - Okta - Modification Feed | 1 |
| Alert - Okta - Kit Health | 1 |

***

## 3. Reading alerts

Alerts raised in the time range, newest first (the alert's fields are under `Contents`;
`Metadata` names the alert and scheduled search). This reports an unknown tag until the
first Okta alert has fired:

```query
tag=_alerts json Contents._vendor==Okta Contents._alert_priority as priority Contents._alert_name as detection Contents.user as user Contents.src_ip as src_ip Contents.context as context Contents.published as published
| sort by published desc
| table published priority detection user src_ip context
```

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `_alert_title` | `Alert: Okta - System Log - <category> - <subcategory> - <priority> - <detection>` |
| `_alert_priority` | Critical (risk ≥ 90), High (≥ 75), Medium (≥ 55), Low (≥ 40), Informational |
| `_alert_name` | the detection; also the key for the tuning lookups |
| `_alert_risk` | risk score, 0-100 |
| `user` | the person acted on if there is one, otherwise the actor |
| `src_ip` | client IP, else the first IP of the request chain |
| `target` | primary target: user acted on > app account > app > first target |
| `eventType`, `published` | from the Okta event |
| `_mitre_tactic`, `_mitre_technique` | ATT&CK mapping |
| `context` | one sentence: who did what to whom, from where, with what result |

The **risk score** starts from a base for the event type (privilege grants 90, API tokens
and identity providers 85, MFA and policy changes 80, ...). Bumps are added for warning or
error severity, a successful outcome, an anonymizing proxy, a privileged actor
(`okta_privileged_actor`) and a protected target (`okta_protected_target`). Many detections
set a minimum priority (the Priority column below).

The **Okta Investigation** dashboard shows an actor's, target's or IP's alerts, events
and sign-ins in one place. The Okta Actor, Okta Target and Okta IP actionables open it
from any search result. The **Playbook - Okta - Alert Response** playbook has the
investigation steps.

***

## 4. Detections

"In the window" = within the 15-minute search window.

"In the window" means within the 15-minute search window.

#### Authentication & Sessions (24)

| Detection | Fires on | Priority | ATT&CK |
|---|---|---|---|
| Attempted Bypass of Okta MFA | `user.mfa.attempt_bypass` | by risk score | Credential Access, T1111 |
| Multiple Okta Sessions for One User from Different IPs | 2+ new sessions from 2+ IPs for one user in the window | Medium minimum | Lateral Movement, T1550.004 |
| Multiple Okta Users Authenticating from the Same Device Behind a Proxy | One device token used by 2+ users through an anonymizing proxy | High minimum | Credential Access, T1110.003 |
| Okta Admin Console Access from a New IP | Admin Console access from an IP the admin has not used for it in 14 days | Medium minimum | Initial Access, T1078.004 |
| Okta Admin Console Access via Anonymizing Proxy | Admin Console access through an anonymizing proxy | High minimum | Initial Access, T1078.004 |
| Okta Brute Force Against One Account | 5+ invalid-credential failures for one user in the window | Medium minimum | Credential Access, T1110.001 |
| Okta FastPass Phishing Detection | FastPass declined to sign in to a phishing origin | by risk score | Initial Access, T1566 |
| Okta Many Device Tokens from One IP on Failed Sign-Ins | 10+ device tokens on invalid-credential failures from one IP in the window | Medium minimum | Credential Access, T1110 |
| Okta New Admin Console Behaviours | Admin Console sign-on with a new device, IP, location or velocity flag | High minimum | Initial Access, T1078.004 |
| Okta OAuth Refresh Token Reuse Detected | Rotated refresh token presented again (org or custom authorization server) | High minimum | Credential Access, T1528 |
| Okta Password Spray from One Device | One device token failing for 3+ users in the window | High minimum | Credential Access, T1110.003 |
| Okta Password Spray from One IP | One IP failing for 3+ users in the window | High minimum | Credential Access, T1110.003 |
| Okta Session Start via Anonymizing Proxy | Session start (success or failure) through an anonymizing proxy | Medium minimum | Command and Control, T1090.003 |
| Okta Session Used from Multiple Countries | One browser session (root session ID) seen from 2+ countries; phone events ignored | High minimum | Credential Access, T1539 |
| Okta Sign-In Events via Third-Party IdP | Inbound IdP / SAML sign-in rejected (replayed or invalid assertion, unknown endpoint) | by risk score | Initial Access, T1199 |
| Okta Sign-In by a Recently Deactivated User | Successful sign-in by a user deactivated or suspended in the previous 30 days and not re-activated | Critical minimum | Initial Access, T1078 |
| Okta Sign-In from a New IP After MFA Reset | Sign-in within 12 hours of an MFA reset, from an IP not used for the reset | High minimum | Initial Access, T1078 |
| Okta Sign-In with Elevated Risk or New Behavior | Okta risk HIGH or Velocity / New Country (High), risk MEDIUM (Medium) | Medium or High minimum | Initial Access, T1078 |
| Okta User Session Impersonation | `user.session.impersonation.initiate` | by risk score | Defense Evasion, T1550 |
| Okta User Sessions Started from Different Geolocations | Sessions for one user from 2+ countries within 4 hours | High minimum | Initial Access, T1078 |
| Potential Okta MFA Bombing via Push Notifications | 5+ pushes, or 3+ pushes with 2+ denied / unanswered, for one user in the window | High minimum | Credential Access, T1621 |
| Potential Okta Password in AlternateID Field | Failed sign-in whose username is not an email address (value masked in the alert) | Medium minimum | Credential Access, T1552 |
| Potentially Successful Okta MFA Bombing via Push Notifications | Push bombing followed by a successful MFA | Critical minimum | Credential Access, T1621 |
| Unauthorized Access to an Okta Application | User tried to open an app they are not assigned to | by risk score | Initial Access, T1078 |

#### Admin & Configuration Changes (13)

| Detection | Fires on | Priority | ATT&CK |
|---|---|---|---|
| Okta API Token Created | `system.api_token.create` | by risk score | Persistence, T1136 |
| Okta API Token Revoked | `system.api_token.revoke` | by risk score | Impact, T1531 |
| Okta Admin Role Assigned to a User or Group | Admin role granted to a user, a group, or through a resource set binding | by risk score | Persistence, T1098.003 |
| Okta Admin Role Granted to a New User | Admin role granted to an account created in the previous 24 hours | Critical minimum | Persistence, T1098.003 |
| Okta Application Modified, Deactivated or Deleted | `application.lifecycle.update/deactivate/delete` | by risk score | Impact, T1489 |
| Okta Application Sign-On Policy Modified or Deleted | `application.policy.sign_on.update/delete` | by risk score | Defense Evasion, T1562 |
| Okta Identity Provider Created | `system.idp.lifecycle.create` | by risk score | Persistence, T1556.007 |
| Okta Network Zone Modified, Deactivated or Deleted | Network zone changed, deactivated, deleted or blocklist entry removed | by risk score | Defense Evasion, T1562 |
| Okta OAuth Client Secret or Credential Accessed or Created | OAuth client secret read; client credential created or activated; client privilege granted | Medium or High minimum | Credential Access, T1552 |
| Okta Policy Modified, Deactivated or Deleted | `policy.lifecycle.update/deactivate/delete` | by risk score | Defense Evasion, T1562 |
| Okta Policy Rule Modified, Deactivated or Deleted | `policy.rule.update/deactivate/delete` | by risk score | Defense Evasion, T1562 |
| Sensitive Okta OAuth Scope Granted to an App | Admin consent for a scope listed in `okta_sensitive_oauth_scopes` (exact match), or any org-level `okta.*.manage` scope | Medium or High minimum | Persistence, T1098.003 |
| Unauthorized Scope for Public App OAuth2 Token Grant with Client Credentials | Client-credentials token request refused with `no_matching_scope` | Medium minimum | Defense Evasion, T1550.001 |

#### Account & MFA Changes (5)

| Detection | Fires on | Priority | ATT&CK |
|---|---|---|---|
| High Number of Okta Password Reset or Unlock Events for One User | 3+ password reset / unlock events for one user in the window | Medium minimum | Credential Access, T1110 |
| Locked Out or Account Locked | Account lockouts (`user.account.lock`, `user.account.lock.limit`) | by risk score | Credential Access, T1110 |
| New Okta User Created | `user.lifecycle.create` | by risk score | Persistence, T1136.003 |
| Okta MFA Reset or Deactivated | MFA factor deactivated or all factors reset | by risk score | Persistence, T1556.006 |
| User Reported Suspicious Activity | End user reported suspicious activity | by risk score | Initial Access, T1078 |

#### Threats & Platform (5)

| Detection | Fires on | Priority | ATT&CK |
|---|---|---|---|
| Okta Protected Action Attempted or Request Blocked | `security.protected_action.attempt`, `security.request.blocked` | Medium minimum | Initial Access, T1078 |
| Okta Security Threat Detected | `security.threat.detected` (ThreatInsight) | by risk score | Credential Access, T1110 |
| Okta Support Access to the Org | Okta Support viewed or changed the org, or was granted impersonation | Medium minimum | Initial Access, T1199 |
| Okta ThreatInsight Threat Suspected Promotion | Any event with `debugData.threatSuspected` true | by risk score | Credential Access, T1110 |
| Possible Okta DoS Attack | Rate-limit and concurrency-limit violations | Medium minimum | Impact, T1498 |

#### Modification Feed (1)

| Detection | Fires on | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| Risk-Scored Modification Feed | Every configuration or account change scoring Low (40) or above; at most 50 events per run | Low and above |

The modification feed is a review queue, not a detection. It overlaps with the Admin &
Configuration Changes detections and is noisier; if you attach a notification to it, expect
more volume than from the other alerts, or disable it if you only want detections.

#### Kit Health (1)

| Detection | Fires on | Priority |
|---|---|---|
| Target Key Order Changed | Okta changed how target objects are serialized (see Known assumptions) | Critical |

***

## 5. Tuning

Lookups (Resources page). Shipped rows are disabled examples (`enabled` = 0). Logins are
lowercase without `@domain`. The detection name is the scheduled search name after the
group, for example `jsmith|Okta Admin Console Access from a New IP`.

| Lookup | Key | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `okta_user_allowlist` | `<login>\|<detection>` or `<login>\|*` | drop alerts for this account as actor or person acted on; optional expiry `until_ymd` |
| `okta_ip_allowlist` | `<ip>\|<detection>` or `<ip>\|*` | drop alerts from this IP; optional expiry |
| `okta_user_denylist` | as above | raise to at least High (optionally only when risk ≥ `min_risk`) |
| `okta_ip_denylist` | as above | raise to at least High (optionally only when risk ≥ `min_risk`) |
| `okta_privileged_actor` | `account` | add `actor_weight` when this account acts |
| `okta_protected_target` | `account` | add `target_weight` when this account is acted on by someone else |
| `okta_sensitive_oauth_scopes` | `scope` | scopes that count as sensitive for "Sensitive Okta OAuth Scope Granted to an App"; tier `None` silences one |
| `okta_eventTypes` | `eventType` | category, subcategory and modification flag |

Thresholds and schedules live in the saved query and scheduled search of each detection.
Upgrading the kit replaces edited items, so keep a note of your changes.

***

## 6. Known assumptions

- **Target serialization.** The kit reads an event's targets by type with a regular
  expression instead of splitting the target list, so each event is one row. This relies on
  Okta writing every target's keys in the order `id, type, alternateId, displayName`.
  **Alert - Okta - Kit Health** fires Critical if an event ever breaks that
  pattern. Typed target fields would then be empty and detections fall back to the first
  target. Report it to the kit maintainers.
- **Window and ingest delay.** Events that arrive after their 15-minute window has been
  searched are not alerted on. If your ingest routinely lags, lengthen the scheduled search
  durations.
- **Okta features.** ThreatInsight, FastPass, behavior detection, risk scoring and support
  access logging must be available in your org for the detections that use them.

***

## 7. What's in the kit

- **Alerts (6)** and **scheduled searches (49)**: see sections 2 and 4.
- **Dashboards (9)**: Okta General Overview, Okta Category Overview, Okta Event Type
  Overview, Okta Actor/Target Overview, Okta Investigation, Okta User Overview, Okta User
  Authentication Overview, Okta Sign-In Locations (heatmap and pointmap of sign-ins,
  sign-ins by country and city, user timezones), and Okta Threat Overview:
  - the alerts the kit raised, by priority and detection;
  - invalid-credential failures by source IP and by user;
  - device tokens shared by several users;
  - Okta Verify push activity;
  - Okta threat signals;
  - risky and new-behavior sign-ins;
  - users in several countries;
  - sign-ins through proxies;
  - password reset and unlock requests;
  - rate-limit violations;
  - a map of failed sign-ins.

  Its tables are ranked, not thresholded, so activity below a detection's threshold shows
  too. Locations come from Okta (`client.geographicalContext`); the queries fall back to
  the `geoip` module only for events Okta didn't place.
- **Templates (20)**: investigation templates (all events, alerts, modification events,
  successful and failed authentication, authentication by country and a sign-in location map
  for an actor, target and/or IP) and event type breakdowns by category, event type, actor/target and result.
- **Actionables (3)**: Okta Actor, Okta Target, Okta IP: open the Investigation dashboard.
- **Saved queries**: one per detection (`Okta - <group> - <detection>`), plus the
  dashboard searches (`Search - Okta - ...`).
- **Macros (2)**: only the config macros `$OKTA` (System Log tag) and `$OKTA_USERS` (user
  profile tag). Every query is self-contained: extraction, enrichment, risk scoring, tuning
  and alert fields are written out in each detection, so a detection can be read and edited
  on its own (an upgrade of the kit replaces your edits).
- **Lookups (8)**: section 5.
- **Aggregates**: the scheduled search **Okta - User Aggregates** saves hourly user-directory
  figures to the resource `okta_user_aggregates` (`ts,metric,key,value,rowkey`). The Okta User Overview and User
  Authentication Overview dashboards and the Sign-In Locations timezone table read only that
  resource. Read a metric with `dump -r okta_user_aggregates metric=="<metric>" key value`, or
  its trend with `dump -r okta_user_aggregates -t ts metric=="<metric>_history" ts key value`.
  See section 1 for what to expect after installing.
- **Autoextractors (2)**: `okta` and `okta-users` for your own `ax` searches on the default
  tags. The kit's queries don't use them.

***

## 8. References

- Okta: [Event types](https://developer.okta.com/docs/reference/api/event-types/),
  [System Log API](https://developer.okta.com/docs/api/openapi/okta-management/management/tag/SystemLog/)
